@page "/render/static"

<PageTitle>Static Render Mode Information</PageTitle>

<h1>Static Render Mode Information</h1>

<p>
    This page explains how static render mode differs from the interactive render modes.
    The current `@@rendermode` directive in .NET 10 supports <code>InteractiveAuto</code>,
    <code>InteractiveServer</code>, and <code>InteractiveWebAssembly</code>.
</p>

<p>
    Static render mode is not exposed as a `@@rendermode` directive value in this version of .NET.
    Instead, static content is best represented by plain Razor pages or pre-rendered content that does not need client-side interactivity.
</p>

<p>
    <strong>Why static-like rendering is chosen in some scenarios:</strong>
    Static rendering is the best choice for pages that are primarily informational and do not require user interaction,
    because it minimizes client-side dependencies and eliminates the need for JavaScript initialization.
</p>

<p>
    <strong>Best cases:</strong>
    marketing pages, documentation, landing pages, and any content-driven view where SEO, fast first paint, and low maintenance are more important than interactivity.
</p>

